libntp: fix inverted sense in the emergency receive-buffer check

Backport of the upstream fix released in ntp 4.2.8p16, ChangeLog:

  * [Bug 3666] avoid unlimited receive buffer allocation <perlinger@ntp.org>
    - follow-up: fix inverted sense in check, reset shortfall counter

4.2.8p15 introduced the receive-buffer limit and the "iron reserve" of
emerg_recvbufs buffers, together with the urgent argument that says whether
a caller may dip into that reserve.  The check got the two cases the wrong
way round: with urgent set the threshold became emerg_recvbufs, and without
it zero, so an urgent caller was refused first and the reserve protected
nothing.  p15 is the only release carrying it.

The visible consequence is a NULL dereference in ntpdate.  input_handler()
decides a buffer is available by testing free_recvbuffs() == 0, then takes
one with get_free_recv_buffer(TRUE) and uses it unchecked:

	if (initializing || free_recvbuffs() == 0) {
		...drop the packet...
		continue;
	}

	rb = get_free_recv_buffer(TRUE);

	fromlen = sizeof(rb->recv_srcadr);
	rb->recv_length = recvfrom(fdc, ...);

With the inverted check those two tests disagree over the whole range
0 < free_recvbufs <= emerg_recvbufs: the guard passes because the free
count is non-zero, the allocator refuses because the count is at or below
the reserve, and ntpdate writes rb->recv_length through NULL.

ntpdate sizes its pool with init_recvbuff(sys_numservers + 2), rounded up
to RECV_INC, so a single-server run owns 32 buffers of which 16 were
unusable.  input_handler() drains the socket in a loop taking one buffer
per packet and returning none, so the 17th reply in a single drain killed
it.  Observed in the field on three radios whose NTP server answered one
request many times over; all three cores are identical, with
total_recvbufs 32, free_recvbufs 16, full_recvbufs 16, emerg_recvbufs 16
and buffer_shortfall 1, faulting on the rb->recv_length store.  Any host
able to duplicate NTP replies could crash ntpdate this way.

With the check corrected, an urgent caller may take buffers down to zero,
which is exactly the case ntpdate's own guard already covers, so the NULL
return becomes unreachable there.  Reserve semantics are restored for
non-urgent callers, which is what "iron reserve for refclocks" meant.

Only the inverted check is backported.  The other half of the upstream
follow-up -- relocating the buffer_shortfall reset and adding LOG_CRIT
diagnostics on pool exhaustion -- is left out on purpose: it adds new
critical-severity log output, and it matters only once the pool reaches
limit_recvbufs, which a short-lived ntpdate run does not approach.  Both
arrive with the eventual package upgrade to 4.2.8p18.

Signed-off-by: Dmitry Grinberg <dmitryg@ceragon.com>
---
--- a/libntp/recvbuff.c
+++ b/libntp/recvbuff.c
@@ -234,7 +234,7 @@
 	recvbuf_t *buffer = NULL;
 
 	LOCK_F();
-	if (free_recvbufs > (urgent ? emerg_recvbufs : 0)) {
+	if (free_recvbufs > (urgent ? 0 : emerg_recvbufs)) {
 		UNLINK_HEAD_SLIST(buffer, free_recv_list, link);
 	}
 	
